\section*{Singer vanishing in dimension four}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
The Singer vanishing assertion is claimed in dimension four, including finite aspherical integral Poincare complexes and nonorientable cases: universal-cover \(L^2\)-Betti numbers vanish in every degree except two. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} Let $Q$ be an integral Poincaré complex that is aspherical, connected and finite, with formal dimension four, allowing an arbitrary orientation character. OpenAI claims
\[
b_j^{(2)}(\widetilde Q)=0\ (j\ne2),\qquad
\chi(Q)=b_2^{(2)}(\widetilde Q)\ge0.
\]
Here $b_j^{(2)}$ is the von Neumann dimension of the homology of $\mathcal N\pi_1(Q)\otimes_{\mathbb Z\pi_1(Q)}C_*(\widetilde Q)$. The claims include closed connected aspherical topological four-manifolds; zero Euler characteristic is equivalent to vanishing of every $L^2$-Betti number. For oriented such manifolds $M$, the manuscript additionally claims $\chi(M)\ge|\sigma(M)|$, where $\sigma$ is the real intersection-form signature. These are dimension-four conclusions.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 315, 2026-09-25): Positive first \(L^2\) cohomology would supply a bounded finite-energy function on a Cayley graph. Its translates define a boundary with controlled approach paths, and annular separation arguments organize cut points into dendritic blocks. Poincare duality imposes dimension and cohomology constraints excluding circles, so a nondegenerate block would be a dendrite with a forbidden cut point.

